---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import coverage from '../data/coverage.json';
import { REPO } from '../lib/site';

const rows = coverage.rows;
const ranDate = (coverage.generated || '').slice(0, 10);
const SAPIS = ['cli', 'fpm', 'apache'] as const;
const SAPI_LABEL: Record<string, string> = { cli: 'CLI server', fpm: 'php-fpm', apache: 'Apache mod_php' };

// Map a per-SAPI cell to a display {pill class, label}. State comes straight
// from the generated data; nothing here invents a verdict.
function cell(sapi: any) {
  if (!sapi || sapi.state === 'none') return { cls: 'na', label: 'not tested' };
  if (sapi.state === 'pass') return { cls: 'ok', label: 'blocked' };
  if (sapi.state === 'skip') return { cls: 'na', label: 'skipped' };
  if (sapi.state === 'fail') return { cls: 'no', label: 'FAILED' };
  return { cls: 'na', label: sapi.state };
}
---
<Layout
  title="Coverage — what Howdah has actually been tested against"
  description="The Howdah test matrix: for every PHP version and server type, whether an upload/part flood was blocked, skipped, or not run. Generated from the lab results, never hand-typed."
  path="/coverage/"
>
  <div class="pagehead">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <span aria-current="page">Coverage</span></p>
      <h1>Coverage: what has actually been tested</h1>
      <p class="lede">Every cell on this page is generated from the lab test matrix{ranDate && <> (run {ranDate})</>}, never typed by hand. A version is only called &ldquo;blocked&rdquo; where the test for that exact version and server type passed.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="totals" role="group" aria-label="Test totals">
        <div><strong>{coverage.totals.pass}</strong><span>checks passed</span></div>
        <div><strong>{coverage.totals.fail}</strong><span>failed</span></div>
        <div><strong>{coverage.totals.skip}</strong><span>skipped</span></div>
        <div><strong>{coverage.corpusFalsePositives}</strong><span>false positives in the legitimate-upload corpus</span></div>
      </div>

      <h2>By PHP version and server type</h2>
      <p>The two flood shapes tested are a <strong>file-part flood</strong> (<code>max_files</code>) and a <strong>field/part flood</strong> (<code>max_parts</code>). &ldquo;Blocked&rdquo; means the shaped request got HTTP 403 and a log entry at the default limits, on a server with raised caps (the exposed setup). The same runs also sent seven ordinary uploads; none was blocked.</p>
      <div class="tablewrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Coverage by PHP version and server type (scrolls sideways on narrow screens)">
        <table>
          <caption class="sr-only">Per-version, per-server-type coverage</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">PHP</th>
              {SAPIS.map((s) => <th scope="col">{SAPI_LABEL[s]}</th>)}
              <th scope="col">Notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {rows.map((r: any) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{r.version ?? r.minor}</th>
                {r.version ? (
                  <>
                    {SAPIS.map((s) => {
                      const c = cell(r.sapis?.[s]);
                      return <td><span class={`pill ${c.cls}`}>{c.label}</span></td>;
                    })}
                    <td><small style="margin:0">{SAPIS.map((s) => r.sapis?.[s]?.note).filter(Boolean).join('; ')}</small></td>
                  </>
                ) : (
                  <>
                    <td colspan="3"><span class="pill na">not run</span></td>
                    <td><small style="margin:0">{r.why}</small></td>
                  </>
                )}
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="legend">Totals: {coverage.totals.pass} passed, {coverage.totals.fail} failed, {coverage.totals.skip} skipped. Generated by <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/site/scripts/gen-coverage.mjs`}>a script</a> from <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/lab/integration/matrix-results.tsv`}>the raw matrix results</a>. The same data in prose is in <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/coverage.md`}>docs/coverage.md</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="runtimes">Other server types and runtimes</h2>
      <p>These are not part of the table above. Each row is a run of one script, recorded in <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/${coverage.runtimeSource}`}>the runtime results</a>: a self-test (a marker request, a request one part over the limit, a normal form), not the full matrix. &ldquo;Passed&rdquo; means Howdah loaded there and refused the over-limit request; &ldquo;failed&rdquo; is why that runtime is not supported.</p>
      <div class="tablewrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Other server types and runtimes (scrolls sideways on narrow screens)">
        <table>
          <caption class="sr-only">Runs outside the per-version matrix</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Runtime</th>
              <th scope="col">CPU</th>
              <th scope="col">PHP</th>
              <th scope="col">Result</th>
              <th scope="col">What ran</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {coverage.runtimes.map((r: any) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{r.runtime}</th>
                <td>{r.arch}</td>
                <td>{r.php.join(', ')}</td>
                <td><span class={`pill ${r.result === 'PASS' ? 'ok' : 'no'}`}>{r.result === 'PASS' ? 'passed' : 'failed'}</span></td>
                <td><small style="margin:0">{r.detail} (<code>{r.script}</code>, {r.date})</small></td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="note">
        <h3>Honest limits of this table</h3>
        <ul class="facts">
          <li>Apache <code>mod_php</code> and php-fpm were both tested on every minor whose cell shows a result — 5.6 and 7.0 through 8.5. PHP 5.4 and 5.5 are the only exceptions (next notes).</li>
          <li>PHP 5.4 was tested on PHP's built-in CLI server only: its stock image has no <code>cgi-fcgi</code> (php-fpm skipped) and there is no <code>php:5.4-apache</code> image.</li>
          <li>{coverage.notRunMinors.length} minors were not independently run: {coverage.notRunMinors.join(', ')}. They share a generation's code path with a version that passed — a reason to expect they work, not a test result, and <strong>not</strong> claimed as passing.</li>
          <li>The CVE's <code>session.upload_progress</code> header-parsing path was <strong>not</strong> reproduced. Upload-progress on and off were both tested, to confirm Howdah works either way.</li>
          <li><strong>CVE-2012-1823 (php-cgi argument injection) is out of reach and not protected:</strong> that query is consumed by php-cgi during its own startup, before any PHP extension loads. It belongs with CVE-2019-11043 — both happen before extension code runs. Use a web-server or front-proxy rule.</li>
          <li>The input-shape and request-header limits are enforced best-effort on the response (the variable is always dropped and logged; the 403 may arrive at request shutdown on lazy-superglobal SAPIs). See <a href="/docs/configuration/">Configuration</a>.</li>
          <li>Layer 2 (input inspection, added in 1.1) is not covered by this table. It detects and logs SQL injection, path traversal and command-injection patterns in request values, is best-effort and can be bypassed, which is why it logs, and it does not block by default.</li>
          <li>Layer 3 (sink hardening, added in 1.2) is not covered by this table either; its per-version table is on the <a href="/#sinks">home page</a>. It guards command execution, <code>unserialize</code>, <code>include</code>/<code>require</code> and <code>eval</code> (the last two on PHP 5.x too since 1.3), plus <code>phar://</code> and SSRF (new in 1.3, both off by default). It detects and logs by default, and does not block unless explicitly armed. It is best-effort and can be bypassed.</li>
          <li><strong>phar (1.3):</strong> on PHP 5.x/7.x it covers phar metadata deserialization through the stream wrapper; on PHP 8.x it is detection and denial of <code>phar://</code> use only, with no deserialization claim. The direct Phar API (<code>new Phar</code>, <code>PharData</code>, <code>Phar::loadPhar</code>, <code>Phar::mapPhar</code>, <code>Phar::getMetadata</code>) is not covered. Another extension re-registering <code>phar</code>, or a static phar build, fails open.</li>
          <li><strong>SSRF (1.3):</strong> best-effort, resolve-at-check-time detection, not a connection-time boundary. It is bypassed by DNS rebinding, redirects, <code>curl_multi_*</code>, <code>CURLOPT_RESOLVE</code>/<code>CONNECT_TO</code> and a non-string or absent <code>CURLOPT_URL</code>. Not hooked: <code>copy</code>, <code>file</code>, <code>readfile</code>, <code>SoapClient</code>, <code>DOMDocument::load*</code>, <code>simplexml_load_file</code>. It adds a <code>getaddrinfo</code> per distinct outbound host with no timeout; more than 32 resolved addresses are capped and a host over 256 bytes is treated as suspicious.</li>
          <li><strong>PHP 5.x <code>include</code> (1.3):</strong> checked at compile time, so a file served from a warm opcache is not re-inspected. <code>eval</code> is unaffected, and the PHP 7.0+ opcode handler is not affected by opcache.</li>
          <li><strong>Performance:</strong> measured latency with and without Howdah, the OPcache JIT check (measured on PHP 8.1, 8.3 and 8.5, before #42; see the next item for the current behaviour) and what Layer 2 flags of the GoTestWAF corpus are on the <a href="/docs/performance/">performance page</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Provenance:</strong> the 1.3 Layer 3 mechanisms were verified on PHP 5.4, 5.6, 7.4, 8.3 and 8.5. Other minors build and are expected to work but are not independently verified. A debug-build leak review is pending.</li>
          <li>Layer 4 (after-breach behaviour, added in 1.4) is not covered by this table. It watches PHP run from denied directories, exec-family calls to unlisted programs, outbound connections to unlisted hosts and PHP files written into denied directories. It detects and logs by default, every list ships empty, and it does not block unless an operator sets <code>behavior.mode = "block"</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Layer 4 sees only PHP.</strong> A process PHP launches and then detaches, a reverse shell that forks away, or a dropper that never ran through PHP is invisible to it.</li>
          <li><strong>Layer 4 <code>deny_exec_dirs</code>:</strong> the requested-script case does not depend on opcache. The <code>require</code>d-file case is checked at first compile and is best-effort (PHP 7.0+: operand string only), so a file served from a warm opcache may not be re-checked. A relative-path <code>require</code> is not checked at include time on any version.</li>
          <li><strong>Layer 4 allowlists</strong> (<code>allow_exec_binaries</code>, <code>allow_egress_hosts</code>) are best-effort and can be bypassed; egress matching shares the Layer 3 SSRF limits above. <code>deny_php_writes</code> is scoped by origin and can still flag frameworks that compile templates into a denied directory; it matches by file extension. <code>observe</code> only proposes, and on an already-breached box its output may include attacker behaviour.</li>
          <li><strong>OPcache JIT:</strong> PHP turns its JIT off whenever an extension installs a user opcode handler, and one of Howdah's two <code>include</code>/<code>eval</code> mechanisms on PHP 7.0+ is one. Nothing is installed unless the <code>eval</code> sink is on, the <code>include</code> sink has <code>allow_paths</code>, or <code>[behavior] deny_exec_dirs</code> is set. The default <code>[sinks] hook = "auto"</code> uses the compile pointer when only <code>eval</code> needs the hook (the shipped default), so <strong>a default install keeps the JIT on</strong>; it uses the opcode handler when <code>include</code> has <code>allow_paths</code> or <code>deny_exec_dirs</code> is set (where an include served from a warm opcache matters) and then writes one startup line, and <code>hook = "compile"</code> keeps the JIT on even then at the cost that a warm-opcache <code>include</code> is not re-inspected (<code>eval</code> is still fully covered). Verified with <code>opcache_get_status()</code> on PHP 8.1 and 8.3 (<code>lab/integration/jit.sh</code>); <code>php --ri howdah</code> shows which mechanism is in use and why.</li>
          <li><strong>Provenance:</strong> the Layer 4 lab script (27 checks) passed on PHP 5.4, 5.6, 7.4, 8.3, 8.4 and 8.5. Other minors are not independently verified.</li>
          <li>The matrix results were recorded on Linux x86_64. aarch64 is build-verified for all 14 minors (PHP 5.4 included since the 1.2.2 build fix); on arm64 only the self-test and block-shaped scripts have run (<a href="#runtimes">other runtimes</a>), not the matrix.</li>
          <li>Howdah is built for NTS PHP only. There is no ZTS build, so FrankenPHP and Apache's threaded MPMs with <code>mod_php</code> are not supported; a test build failed under concurrent requests (<a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/zts.md`}>docs/zts.md</a>).</li>
          <li>Under php-fpm, refusing an over-limit multipart request ends the worker process (exit code 70), and behind nginx the 403 usually reaches the client without the Howdah message; the event is logged either way. See <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/install-docker.md`}>docs/install-docker.md</a>.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .totals { display: grid; gap: 1rem; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); margin: 0 0 2rem; }
  @media (min-width: 40rem) { .totals { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); } }
  .totals > div { background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 10px; padding: 1rem 1.1rem; }
  .totals strong { display: block; font: 700 2rem/1 var(--display); color: var(--hot); }
  .totals span { font-size: .85rem; color: var(--muted); }
</style>
